Thank you for your time and effort for reviewing our work. We sincerely appreciate your comments highlighting the novelty and insightfulness of our neuron-level analysis using Concept Activation Vectors (CAVs), as well as your view that our findings offer concrete insights into how prompts shape LLM representations and benefit the broader research community. We are also grateful for your constructive suggestions on additional neuron-level analyses that could further strengthen our research.


> **W1. The experiments mainly rely on value vectors to verify hypotheses, while value neurons are only used as motivation. I would have liked to see experiments exploring which concepts the shared neurons correspond to, and what kinds of meanings are captured by unique neurons (intrinsic) and unique neurons (prompted). Recent works have shown progress in automatically generating neuron explanations. The absence of such analysis here is somewhat disappointing — if the authors could include experiments visualizing or describing these neuron-level concepts, I would consider increasing my score.**

**A.** Thank you for your suggestions! This is a great idea, so we conducted additional experiments based on [1] and [2] to analyze the specific roles of the neurons that we identified. 

Specifically, we fed a large corpus (50,000 random excerpts of 64 tokens from OpenWebText [3]) through the model and recorded the top-10 highest activating excerpts for each neuron. After that, we provided those examples to an explainer model (GPT-4o-mini) based on the summary prompt format from [2]. For each of the ten Schwartz values, we selected, from each group (shared, unique‑intrinsic, unique‑prompted), the neurons with the largest L2 norm in the SVD subspace defined in our paper (see Section 2.2), and inspected the automatically generated explanations. 

Surprisingly, we found that many of those neurons correspond to meaningful and interpretable features. We have submitted an updated version of the manuscript (see Section 4.1, 4.2, and Appendix K) that includes an extensive list of these neuron-level descriptions for all models. A concise summary of the representative patterns for Qwen2.5-7b-Instruct model is provided below.

**Shared neurons:**
These consistently encode abstract, high-level features central to each value.
For example, 
- a Tradition shared neuron at [Layer 14, Neuron 587] fires on mentions of spiritual or religious practices and group rituals;

- The Conformity/Power shared neuron [L11, N15699] fires to social approval, sanction, and collective restraint language;

- For Security, a shared neuron at [L14, N817] responds to institutional risk or safety concepts (like “overload”, “shattering”, “emergency”);

- For Universalism, a neuron at [L13, N1954] is activated by societal ideals and collective welfare;

- Benevolence, [L12, N2456], responds to positive social impact and community well-being.

In all cases, these shared neurons map closely onto the core semantics of each value in Schwartz’s theory. Importantly, they do not merely respond to specific keywords; they generalize to diverse linguistic realizations of each value.

**Prompted-unique neurons:** These fire most strongly for explicit value definitions and keywords. 

- For Security, the prompted-unique neuron [L14, N4228] activates on explicit terms like “danger”, “warning”, “threat”;
- In Achievement, [L12, N7214] is responsive to “growth”, “overcoming”, “improvement”, etc.;
- For Tradition, [L13, N1047] activates for “heritage”, “legacy”, and “preservation”;
- In Stimulation, [L13, N3872] is triggered by “adventure”, “thrill”, “exciting”, and similar keywords.

Prompted-unique neurons tend to activate for specific value-related keywords or phrases. We speculate that this arises because prompted behaviors are guided by value-inducing system instructions, causing the model to focus on terms and expressions closely associated with the prompted value. This pattern is consistent with our broader observations: when using prompted value vectors for steering, the model has stronger tendencies to repeat specific keywords (Section 3.2.2, Section 4.2).
